  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1044 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nA west-east oriented mid/upper ridge of high pressure into the \ncentral Plains will help keep the area of low pressure in Texas \nmostly south of the Red River as it moves eastward through\nTuesday. During the day Tuesday looks to be the closest it reaches\nto southeast Oklahoma before getting a push southeast from a more\ndefined low pressure system approaching the Plains. An increase \nin low level Theta-e moisture and marginal warm advection into \nsoutheast Oklahoma could create a few sprinkles Tuesday afternoon \nbefore the low begins to depart the region. For now will continue \nto keep PoPs below mentionable criteria for far southeast \nOklahoma. Tuesday looks to be the warmest day of the week with the\nreturn of southerly winds. \n\nThe stronger low pressure system drops southeast into the Plains\nTuesday night and moves across the CWA Wednesday. An associated\ncold front also makes its way into northeast Oklahoma early\nWednesday morning and exits during the afternoon hours. Majority\nof the moisture is forecast to remain south and east of the CWA\nWednesday, with any storm development along the front initiating\neast of the CWA. Behind the frontal passage, breezy\nnorth/northwesterly winds transport drier air and a cooler airmass\ninto the region for the second half of the week. A secondary area\nof low pressure is progged to quickly drop through the Plains and\nthe eastern half of the CWA within the upper level northerly flow\nThursday afternoon/evening. The combination of these features will\nprovide high temperatures back in the 50s/60s and lows in the\n30s/40s Thursday into the weekend.",
